Z||000053||Mach10||04-10-2001||10:19 PM||||120 Down Portage? That's plain STUPID. baldlobo: Tell your brother that he's a stupid corpse-felching ass-bandit. I hope he gets into a SERIOUS accident, and does us the favor of removing himself from the gene pool.<P>I don't ever do more than 15 over the speed limit in the city. Ever seen what happens when a pedestrian gets hit? I've seen 5 of those, all by speeding assholes. Saw a Dad pulling his daughter get smacked by a corpse-felching ricer that blew a PD crossing. Right in front of a cop. Funny watching his white-poser ass get hauled off in cuffs. Lucky the dude was just bruised, and the baby OK.<P>Gonna open'er up, take it on the highway for chrissake. Yes. The Perimeter is ideal for this. Just watch for Smokie-da-bears.<P>Fastest ever gone -190Kph My 92 Mazda Protege<BR>Fastest car - 2001 Porsche Boxster. Was brand new, with owner, so didn't go AFAP. Had it to 150 in about 15-20 seconds. What a rush. The sound that thing makes is soooooo nice  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||8
Z||000054||JSocha||04-11-2001||11:06 AM||||Child Passenger back in about 1977:<P>1960-70 something Cadillac Silver Ghost (thats what my friends family called it).<P>Friends dad was a lawyer and on the EMT.<P>My friends dad got a call while we were outside playing, he grabbed us, threw us in the car, went to the hospital emergency door, came out with a cooler and we were all headed off to the airport where we met a scraggly old guy (greased head to toe I swear...looked like he hadn't bathed since birth).  Got there, got in my friends dad's plane and it wouldn't start (Cessna).  <P>He then yelled at us to get out as quickly as possible and get back in the car.<P>In the meantime he and the scraggly old guy went into the airport office, came out a few minutes later, got in the car and went back to my friends house.<P>We were told to stay in the back seat.<P>Next thing I know, they came out of the house, shoved a mattress in the back seat between us and the front seats, threw a bunch of blankets and pillows on top of us after they fastened the hell out of the seatbelts (my waist hurt after that trip for about a month) and made sure they were stuffed real tight between us and the mattress (at that time getting a little scared we was cause we both B just knee high to a grasshopper at that time).<P>My friends dad again yelled at us to sit there and not to remove anything no matter what.  &lt;inner thought...ohhhhhkaaayyyy!&gt;<P>Next thing I know we were being escourted out of town by the police where soon they were behind us and not in front of us anymore and then gone.<P>I remember police in every town we went through with their lights on before we got to town and for a short time after until we got to the major highway where they had the traffic stopped.<P>Anyhow, we got to Fargo where we were escourted to I think it was the Dakota Clinic.<P>I remember on the trip down the scraggly guy talking about that he hopes none of the tires give out, otherwise they won't have to worry about just one body part but would have others to pick from where he turned to us and asked us if we would give up our body parts if we was dead.  (okay...now I have to suddenly go to the biffy...cause now there is something definately wrong here that I think we should be concerened about).<P>I was on the passenger side in the back, with this cooler in the middle of us all duct taped up, us (or at least me) sweting like hell under all the blankets and looking at the speedometer between the seats and seeing this needle to the far right and wonderin "what b happenin?".  Don't know if it was 120MPH speedo or 160MPH and neither does my friend.<P>I talked with my friend years later when we were both suit mates at college and we talked about that day (as his father had passed away in 1986 while I was in college and we were remennissing about the days we were younger).<P>Learned that somebody had either crashed (motorcycle or car) or had committed suicide (he was unsure of the actual details) and time was critical as they had to get the body part to Fargo where it would be transported to Rochester, MN.  I guess his dad was going to fly it all the way, but when the plane didn't start, they made other arrangements to get it to Fargo.  <P>Well, he talked about how his dad and the other guy had roughly calculated their speed the day after to be 180+ MPH as it had taken us only 78 minutes to travel 240-250 miles.  <P>I know it takes me between 3 1/2 to 4 hours to drive that exact same route we took then at 65 MPH today.<P>Any mathmaticians out there?  <P>I flunked any and all of my math courses and only found "algebra" (what I could remember of it) to be good only when your out for pizza with friends and wonderin how many piecies you can eat without looking like a pig!  5X=14<P>I remember it took us a lot longer to get back.  4 some hours at the service station getting the oil changed and checking everything else out on the vehicle from front to back (including TIRES...the old scraggly guy was curious); and the 4 hour drive gettin back home.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fiero5a_3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
